 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be showing **different measures**, so the labels aren’t necessarily contradictory. If by PSI you mean Singapore’s Pollutant Standards Index, **“Moderate” describes air quality averaged over the past 24 hours**. Your app may instead be showing a more recent reading, a nearby sensor, or an AQI calculated using different methods. A short-lived pollution spike can therefore trigger an app warning while the 24-hour PSI remains Moderate. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/readers-letters/index/pm-2-5-for-immediate-activities-psi-forecast-for-next-day?utm_source=openai))\n\nCheck the **time, location, pollutant, and index name** beside each reading. For a decision about going outside *right now* in Singapore, look at the **1-hour PM₂.₅ reading alongside the PSI**; the PSI alone may not reflect a recent change. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block&utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you tell me the app and the two readings, I can help pinpoint the difference.",
